\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Spectral decomposition of eight candidate initial states in a $10 \times 10$ lattice. Each \textbf  {panel} analyzes a specific initial condition and consists of three elements: (1) a schematic illustration of the initial photon distribution (left), where yellow cells indicate nodes with a non-zero initial photon probability, serving as a binary visual guide independent of exact statistical weights or quantum phases; (2) a bar chart of the mode contribution weights $W_k=|c_k|^2$ (eq. \ref {eq:c_k}) plotted on a \textbf  {logarithmic scale} (top right); and (3) spatial population maps of the three dominant eigenmodes providing the largest contribution to the expansion (bottom right). Notably, for the coherent diagonal state (panel 5), the dominant contributions arise from very high-order modes whose local populations fall below the visual color scale threshold, rendering their maps effectively blank (indicated by the \textit  {<}\textit  {<0.01} label).}}{10}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:9states}{{2}{10}{Spectral decomposition of eight candidate initial states in a $10 \times 10$ lattice. Each \textbf {panel} analyzes a specific initial condition and consists of three elements: (1) a schematic illustration of the initial photon distribution (left), where yellow cells indicate nodes with a non-zero initial photon probability, serving as a binary visual guide independent of exact statistical weights or quantum phases; (2) a bar chart of the mode contribution weights $W_k=|c_k|^2$ (eq. \ref {eq:c_k}) plotted on a \textbf {logarithmic scale} (top right); and (3) spatial population maps of the three dominant eigenmodes providing the largest contribution to the expansion (bottom right). Notably, for the coherent diagonal state (panel 5), the dominant contributions arise from very high-order modes whose local populations fall below the visual color scale threshold, rendering their maps effectively blank (indicated by the \textit {<}\textit {<0.01} label)}{figure.caption.3}{}}
